      <h3>Working Safely at the Wheel</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The SG-250 turns at about 90 rpm under water. Tormek&rsquo;s instructions repeatedly say to press with the fingers <em>close to the edge</em>; that is safe here in a way it would never be on a dry grinder, and it is how the slow wheel is made to cut.</li>
        <li>With the universal support in the <strong>vertical</strong> sleeves the wheel runs <em>into</em> the edge. Keep the jig pressed down on the bar at all times; a lifted jig lets the edge catch and the tool can be thrown. Skews and scrapers above about 60&deg; are done in the horizontal position for this reason.</li>
        <li><strong>Hone only with the edge trailing.</strong> An edge presented into the leather cuts the wheel and can grab the tool.</li>
        <li>Do not hone small exchangeable cutters on the leather wheel; they catch.</li>
        <li>HSS turning tools cannot be overheated on the wet wheel. If any initial shaping is done on a dry grinder (Tormek sells the BGM-100 mount for its jigs), grind lightly at the very tip and never let it blue; quenching a hot edge in water causes invisible micro-cracks.</li>
        <li>Keep the stone true and the trough filled; a dry stone or a grooved one gives an unpredictable grind and a hotter edge.</li>
      </ul>

