      <h2>How the TTS-100 Makes Grinds Repeatable</h2>
      <p>Three things fix the geometry of a gouge or skew once it is in a jig: the <strong>jig setting</strong> (JS &mdash; the click number on the SVD-186 R or the skew angle on the SVS-50 seat), the <strong>protrusion</strong> (P &mdash; how far the tool sticks out of the jig), and the <strong>distance from the universal support to the wheel</strong>. Repeat all three and the wheel meets the bevel at exactly the same angle and the same sweep every time, regardless of how much the stone has worn. The TTS-100 is simply a gauge for the second and third numbers so that they can be set in seconds without measuring anything.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tts100_p02_01.jpg" alt="Diagram of the three factors for the SVD-186 R and SVS-50: jig setting, protrusion P, and distance to the grinding wheel set with the TTS-100 in hole A or B" />
        <figcaption>Figure 2-1: Jig setting, protrusion and support distance &mdash; the three numbers that define a grind</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>The Two Sides of the Setter</h3>
      <p><strong>Side 1</strong> has two round holes marked A and B that drop over the universal support bar, two steel contact discs along the lower edge, and a printed table of the standard profiles. Hole A is the outer hole and puts the support farther from the stone (larger edge angle for a given protrusion); hole B is the inner hole and puts it closer (smaller angle). To set the support, slip the chosen hole over the bar, swing the setter down onto the wheel until <em>both</em> contact discs touch the stone, and lock the support there. Because the setter registers off the wheel surface itself, the geometry comes out the same on a fresh 250 mm stone, a worn one, or a 200 mm wheel.</p>
      <p><strong>Side 2</strong> is a stepped protrusion gauge with stops at 75, 65 and 55 mm. With the jig loosely holding the tool, push the jig flange against one step and the tool tip against the back until both touch, then clamp. Three notches along one edge (30&deg;, 45&deg;, 55&deg;) are a quick check of an existing edge angle, and two magnets let the setter park on the machine.</p>
