      <h3>Shaping Once, Sharpening Forever</h3>
      <p>Tormek draws a hard line between <em>shaping</em> and <em>sharpening</em>. Shaping means grinding a tool to one of the setter&rsquo;s geometries for the first time; it removes real steel and takes 10&ndash;20 minutes on the SG-250. Sharpening means putting the tool back into the same jig at the same numbers and touching the bevel for 10&ndash;20 seconds. A new tool almost never arrives at a chart geometry, so budget the shaping session once, write the numbers on a PL-01 label, stick the label on the ferrule, and never change the profile again. If a different grind is wanted for different work, Tormek&rsquo;s advice is to buy a second tool rather than re-shape back and forth.</p>
      <div class="note">
        There is no need to color the bevel when the setter is used &mdash; the setter already puts the support where the whole bevel meets the wheel. The marker method (below) is for tools that are being sharpened to a shape that is <em>not</em> on the chart.
      </div>

