      <h3>Beyond the Chart: Jig Setting vs Edge Angle</h3>
      <p>The jig setting controls how far the wings sweep back for a given edge angle; the support distance controls the edge angle itself. Tormek&rsquo;s matrix below shows what a bowl gouge looks like at every click from 0 to 6 across four edge angles, with P = 65 mm and a full 180&deg; swing. The two starred cells are the setter&rsquo;s own Bowl 2 and Bowl 4 profiles. A higher JS number tips the tool further over as it rotates, which lengthens the wings; a lower number keeps the grind closer to a traditional straight-across bevel.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p11_02.jpg" alt="Matrix of bowl gouge profiles for jig settings 0 through 6 at edge angles of 35, 45, 55 and 75 degrees; JS 2 at 45 degrees and JS 4 at 55 degrees are marked as the TTS-100 geometries" />
        <figcaption>Figure 2-4: Bowl gouge shapes by jig setting and edge angle (P = 65 mm, full swing)</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h2>Bowl Gouges &mdash; SVD-186 R</h2>
      <p>A bowl gouge is a deep-fluted gouge with a bevel that may run straight across the nose (a &ldquo;traditional&rdquo; grind, roughly Bowl 1 with limited swing) or sweep back along the wings so that the sides of the flute cut as well as the nose (the fingernail, Irish, side or Ellsworth grind &mdash; Bowl 2, 3 and 5). The Hurricane 5/8&quot; HTT-242SGK comes with a factory side grind and belongs at Bowl 2, 45&deg; with a full swing. The Sorby 3/8&quot; is kept at Bowl 1 for finishing cuts, with Bowl 4 (55&deg;) as the option if it ends up dedicated to the bottoms of deep bowls.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/web_svd186r_inuse.jpg" alt="Bowl gouge mounted in the SVD-186 R on the universal support, being swung across the grindstone" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/web_svd186r_tool.jpg" alt="SVD-186 R gouge jig with a gouge clamped in it, seen from the side" />
      </div>

      <h3>Jig Anatomy</h3>
      <p>The tool holder (1) is a sleeve with a clamp screw (5) and an alignment disc (4) that seats in the flute and squares the gouge so that it goes back in the same way every time &mdash; <strong>flute up, toward the disc</strong>. The holder rides in a sleeve (2) that pivots on the universal support; the click ring (6) sets JS 0&ndash;6, and a knurled stop ring (7) with its screw (8) limits how far the holder can slide forward. The locking knob (3) is new on the R version and freezes the pivot, which is handy for grinding a straight-across bevel or a V-tool. The shaft (11) with its screw (12) replaces the holder when sharpening round cutters.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/svd186_p03_01.jpg" alt="SVD-186 R exploded drawing with numbered parts: tool holder, sleeve, locking knob, disc, screw, click setting, stop ring, stop screw, profile label, pen, cutter shaft, screw and hex key" />
        <figcaption>Figure 3-1: SVD-186 R components (1 tool holder, 2 sleeve, 3 locking knob, 4 disc, 5 clamp screw, 6 click setting, 7 stop ring, 8 stop screw, 9 label, 10 pen, 11&ndash;13 cutter shaft, screw, key)</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Settings</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Pull the universal support out of the horizontal base and drop it into the <strong>vertical sleeves</strong> above the grindstone. Gouges are ground toward the edge: water runs over the edge and the wheel presses the jig down onto the support.</li>
        <li>Turn the click ring to the JS number for the profile (2 for Bowl 1&ndash;3, 4 for Bowl 4, 6 for Bowl 5) and tighten it firmly &mdash; it must not creep during the grind.</li>
        <li>Slide the gouge into the holder flute-up, snug the screw lightly, and set the protrusion on side 2 of the TTS-100: jig flange against the 65 (or 75) step, tip against the far wall. Tighten the clamp screw hard.</li>
        <li>Hang hole <strong>A</strong> of the setter on the support bar, rock the setter until both steel discs sit on the stone, and lock the support.</li>
        <li>Write JS / P / A on a profile label and stick it on the ferrule.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/svd186_p04_01.jpg" alt="Three settings panels for a gouge: jig setting on the click ring, protrusion against the TTS-100 stops, and setter in hole A over the support with discs on the stone; fourth panel shows a profile label being written on the ferrule" />
        <figcaption>Figure 3-2: The three settings, and recording them on the ferrule label</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Shaping a New Profile</h3>
      <p>Expect to remove a fair amount of steel the first time. Grade the stone <strong>coarse</strong> with the SP-650 and work with real pressure &mdash; a thumb pressed on the tool close to the edge is what makes the slow wheel cut; it is not a dry grinder and the edge cannot blue.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Grind <strong>one wing at a time</strong>. Rotate the jig so the left wing sits on the stone and work it, moving the jig side to side along the support so the whole width of the stone wears rather than one track.</li>
        <li>Rotate to the right wing and do the same. Lift and look often; where one wing is longer, grind the other more.</li>
        <li>Check the <strong>wing profile</strong> from the side. The wings should be straight or slightly convex. If they go hollow (concave), grind more at the nose &mdash; that is where the steel is, and the wings follow.</li>
        <li>How far back the wings reach depends only on <strong>how far you swing</strong>: a limited swing gives Bowl 1, a full 180&deg; swing at the same settings gives Bowl 2.</li>
        <li>As the shape approaches, stop and re-check the <strong>protrusion</strong> against the setter. Shaping shortens the tool; if P has crept below 65 mm, loosen, re-set it, and finish the shape at the correct protrusion so that future sharpenings land on the same geometry.</li>
        <li>Ease off the pressure and finish with light, continuous swings across the whole bevel to blend the two sides into a single facet-free surface.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p10_01.jpg" alt="Six panels of shaping a bowl gouge: grinding the left side, the right side, checking where to grind more so both wings are symmetrical, and swinging across the stone" />
        <figcaption>Figure 3-3: Shaping one side at a time, checking symmetry, then blending with a full swing</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p10_02.jpg" alt="Four gouge profiles: limited swing versus full swing for wing length, and convex versus concave wing shape with concave crossed out" />
        <figcaption>Figure 3-4: Wing length comes from swing; wings must be straight or convex, never concave</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="warning">
        Grinding too long in one spot grooves the SG-250. Keep the jig moving along the support. Shallow grooves wear out on the next few flat grinds; a badly grooved stone needs the TT-50.
      </div>

      <h3>Rounding the Heel</h3>
      <p>Many turners take the sharp heel off a bowl gouge bevel so it does not bruise the inside of a bowl. Slacken the stop screw (8), slide the stop ring (7) and sleeve back so the holder can travel forward, and push the jig toward the stone while sliding it back and forth to roll the heel off. Moving the support a touch closer to the stone makes a more pronounced radius; leaving the jig stationary instead gives a flat secondary bevel. Put the stop ring back afterwards &mdash; it is what keeps the protrusion honest during a normal sharpening.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p11_01.jpg" alt="Three panels: jig in its normal position, stop ring and sleeve moved back, and the jig pressed toward the wheel to round the heel" />
        <figcaption>Figure 3-5: Freeing the stop ring to round the heel</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Re-sharpening</h3>
      <p>This is the part that is supposed to be fast. Read the label, set JS, set P on the setter, set the support with hole A, and swing the gouge across the stone with light pressure for 10&ndash;20 seconds. Grade the stone <strong>fine</strong> first if the edge only needs refreshing; leave it coarse if the edge has a chip or has been dropped. Feel for a burr along the whole edge from wing to wing; when it is continuous, the bevel has reached the edge everywhere and you are done grinding.</p>

      <h3>Honing the Bevel and the Flute</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Leave the gouge in the jig. Move the universal support to the <strong>horizontal sleeves on the honing-wheel side</strong>, and set it with the setter in hole A exactly as for grinding (the LA-220 leather wheel is the same diameter as a new stone, so the geometry carries over).</li>
        <li>Apply a thin bead of PA-70 compound to the leather if it looks dry. Swing the gouge across the flat honing wheel, edge trailing, with light pressure until the bevel shows a mirror band at the edge.</li>
        <li>Take the gouge out of the jig and present the flute to the <strong>LA-120</strong> profiled wheel so the flute is tangent to the disc. Rock it from wing to wing a few strokes to peel off the burr and polish the inside. Alternate bevel and flute once more if any burr remains.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p11_03.jpg" alt="Three honing panels: setter in hole A on the honing-wheel side, gouge in jig being swung on the flat leather wheel, and the flute held against the LA-120 profiled wheel" />
        <figcaption>Figure 3-6: Honing the bevel in the jig, then the flute on the LA-120</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Checking the Result</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Sight down the flute: the nose and both wings should form one continuous curve with no corners where the wing meets the nose.</li>
        <li>Look at the bevel in raking light: a single clean facet from heel to edge. A second shiny line partway up means the support moved or JS was wrong.</li>
        <li>Thumbnail across (never along) the edge: it should catch evenly at the nose and at both wings.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Common Faults</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Facets or a double bevel</strong> &mdash; protrusion or support position not reproduced; re-set with the setter and blend with a full swing.</li>
        <li><strong>Wings ground back too far</strong> (very long, thin wings that flex and catch) &mdash; too much swing or too high a JS during shaping. Drop to a lower JS or limit the swing and let the nose catch up over the next several sharpenings; do not try to fix it in one session.</li>
        <li><strong>Concave wings</strong> &mdash; too much time on the wings and not enough at the nose. Grind the nose.</li>
        <li><strong>Edge angle drifts steeper as the stone wears</strong> &mdash; only happens if the support is set by eye. Use the setter every time.</li>
        <li><strong>Burr will not come off</strong> &mdash; hone the flute, not just the bevel.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="video-row">
        <div class="video">
          <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/e4tvegeMEC4" title="How to sharpen bowl and spindle gouges – Tormek SVD-186 Gouge Jig – with Nick Agar" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
          <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>How to sharpen bowl and spindle gouges &ndash; SVD-186 &ndash; with Nick Agar</em> (7:54)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="video">
          <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/f6-84PlgFwo" title="Gouge sharpening - From factory grind to a superior sharpness" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
          <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>Gouge sharpening &ndash; from factory grind to a superior sharpness</em> (3:30)</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Spindle Gouges &mdash; SVD-186 R</h2>
      <p>Spindle gouges are shallower in the flute and ground to a longer, pointier fingernail than a bowl gouge so the nose can get into coves and beads. The setter offers two: <strong>Spindle 2</strong> (45&deg;, JS 2, P 65, hole A) as the everyday profile, and <strong>Spindle 1</strong> (30&deg;, JS 2, P 55, hole <em>B</em>) for fine detail, where the short protrusion and the inner hole combine to give the acute angle. Both Sorby gouges start at Spindle 2; the 1/4&quot; is the candidate for Spindle 1 if it ends up as a detail-only tool.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p12_01.jpg" alt="Spindle gouge settings panels: click ring at JS 2, protrusion against the TTS-100 stops, setter over the support in hole A or B" />
        <figcaption>Figure 4-1: Spindle gouge settings &mdash; JS 2 for both profiles; P and hole differ</figcaption>
      </div>
      <ol>
        <li>Support vertical, grinding toward the edge, exactly as for bowl gouges.</li>
        <li>JS 2. Mount flute-up against the disc; protrusion 65 mm (Spindle 2) or 55 mm (Spindle 1) off the setter.</li>
        <li>Setter in hole A (Spindle 2) or hole B (Spindle 1), both discs on the stone, lock.</li>
        <li>Shape one side at a time as for a bowl gouge, swinging the full 180&deg; so the wings run well back. Keep the wings straight or convex. The nose of a spindle gouge is small, so stop frequently; it is easy to grind past the point.</li>
        <li>Sharpen with the stone graded <strong>fine</strong> &mdash; these tools take fine work and benefit from the finer edge, and so little steel comes off that the coarse surface is unnecessary.</li>
        <li>Hone the bevel in the jig on the LA-220 with the support set by the setter on the honing side, then the flute on the LA-120. The 1/4&quot; flute is narrow enough that the 60&deg; pointed disc fits better than the 3 mm radius disc.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="note">
        Spindle 1 and Spindle 2 differ in P <em>and</em> hole. If a tool is swapped between them it will need a 10-minute reshape, not a touch-up. Pick one per tool and label it.
      </div>

      <h2>Spindle Roughing Gouges &mdash; SVS-50 Open Seat</h2>
      <p>A roughing gouge is ground square across the end with a single bevel of about 45&deg;. There is no fingernail and no swing, so it goes in the SVS-50 with the <strong>open seat</strong>, which clamps the tool from the top and leaves the shank free to roll on the support. The Sorby 3/4&quot; (19 mm) fits easily; the open seat takes gouges up to 50 mm.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/svs50_p01_01.jpg" alt="SVS-50 Multi Jig on the universal support with a skew, beside the list of tools it takes: square and skew chisels up to 32 mm, parting and beading tools, roughing gouges up to 50 mm, and carving gouges and chisels" />
        <figcaption>Figure 5-1: The SVS-50 and the tools it covers</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/svs50_p01_02.jpg" alt="SVS-50 housing with numbered parts: housing, closed seat, open seat, side screw, top screw and bottom locking screw" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p08_01.jpg" alt="Closed seat holding a skew with the side screw, and open seat holding a tool with the top screw, with the bottom screw locking the skew angle" />
      </div>
      <h3>Settings</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Fit the open seat and lock it at <strong>0&deg;</strong> with the bottom screw (6), with the seat resting against its stop (7).</li>
        <li>Put the universal support in the <strong>horizontal base in front of the grindstone</strong>. The wheel runs away from the edge; the rotation pulls the jig down onto the bar and there is no risk of the edge digging in.</li>
        <li>Mount the gouge flute-up with <strong>75 mm</strong> protrusion off the setter and clamp it with the top screw (5).</li>
        <li>Set the edge angle with the <strong>marker method</strong>, aiming for roughly 45&deg; on a new tool or matching the existing bevel. The WM-200 can be used instead if you want a specific number.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>Grinding</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Keep the whole flange of the jig flat on the support bar &mdash; if one corner lifts, the angle changes.</li>
        <li><strong>Roll</strong> the gouge about its own axis on the support so the bevel sweeps from one side of the flute to the other, while at the same time sliding the jig along the bar so the stone wears evenly. Light hand pressure near the edge, thumb on the flute.</li>
        <li>Carry on until a burr can be felt along the entire edge from corner to corner. With the stone graded coarse this is a minute or two from a dull edge; graded fine, a few passes.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>Honing</h3>
      <p>Move the support to the honing-wheel side with the tool still in the jig, set the same angle with the marker method, and roll the bevel across the LA-220 until it is bright. Then take the gouge out and pass the flute over the LA-120. The gap between the two discs is wide enough for a 32 mm roughing gouge, so the 3/4&quot; fits with room to spare.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p12_02.jpg" alt="Roughing gouge sequence: locking the seat at zero degrees, setting 75 mm protrusion on the setter, setting the support with the marker method, rolling the gouge on the support, flange flat on the bar, feeling for a burr, honing in the jig, and honing the flute on the LA-120" />
        <figcaption>Figure 5-2: Roughing gouge &mdash; settings, sharpening and honing</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/cXvMLvGADQk" title="Sharpening a roughing gouge" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>Sharpening a roughing gouge</em> (3:14)</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Skew Chisels &mdash; SVS-50 Closed Seat</h2>
      <p>A skew has two identical bevels meeting at an included angle &alpha;, with the edge set over at a skew angle to the shank &mdash; 20&deg; on the setter&rsquo;s profiles. The edge can be straight (Skew 1 and 2) or ground to a gentle convex curve (Skew 3 and 4), the shape popularized by Richard Raffan that keeps the long point out of trouble in a planing cut. The <strong>closed seat</strong> is the key: it holds the tool on its centerline, so flipping the whole jig over grinds the second bevel as an exact mirror of the first without re-mounting.</p>
      <p>In the rack: the Sorby 3/4&quot; flat skew and the Sorby 1/2&quot; oval skew go at <strong>Skew 2</strong> (45&deg; included, straight, P 55). The Hurricane 1&quot; already has a radiused edge and rolled (rounded) long edges, so it goes straight to <strong>Skew 4</strong> (45&deg;, JS 30&deg;, P 65). The 1/2&quot; oval is right at the closed seat&rsquo;s 13 mm minimum; make sure it is seated on center before tightening.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/web_svs50_tool1.jpg" alt="Flat skew chisel clamped on center in the closed seat of the SVS-50" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/web_svs50_tool2.jpg" alt="SVS-50 Multi Jig holding a turning tool, viewed from the front" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/web_svs50.jpg" alt="SVS-50 Multi Jig studio photo showing the swivelling seat and angle scale" />
      </div>

      <h3>Before the First Grind: Round the Corners</h3>
      <p>A flat (rectangular) skew should have its long edges rounded before it is shaped the first time. Rounded corners slide on the lathe rest without catching, and they also let the tool settle on center in the closed seat. Do it freehand on the side of the stone and polish on the leather wheel. The Hurricane rolled-edge skew already has this; the Sorby 3/4&quot; may need it.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p13_03.jpg" alt="Cross-section of a flat skew with arrows showing the four corners to round off" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p13_01.jpg" alt="Universal support horizontal with the wheel running away from the edge, and vertical with the wheel running toward the edge" />
      </div>

      <h3>Support Position</h3>
      <p>Horizontal base, wheel running <strong>away</strong> from the edge, is the normal position: the wheel pulls the jig onto the bar, no water floods the edge, and the pressure is light enough to control a narrow tool. For heavy reshaping the vertical sleeves (toward the edge) remove steel faster.</p>
      <div class="warning">
        In the vertical position the wheel is driving into the edge. Keep the jig pressed firmly down on the support at all times or the edge will catch the stone. Do all routine sharpening in the horizontal position.
      </div>

      <h3>Settings</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Fit the <strong>closed seat</strong>. Swivel it to <strong>20&deg;</strong> on the scale for a straight edge or <strong>30&deg;</strong> for a radius edge and lock hard with the bottom screw (6). The top screw (5) is not used and can be removed.</li>
        <li>Slide the skew into the seat and clamp with the side screw (4) at the protrusion from the chart &mdash; 65 mm (Skew 1), 55 mm (Skew 2), 75 mm (Skew 3) or 65 mm (Skew 4) &mdash; set on side 2 of the setter. The setter&rsquo;s P stops are made for the current 52 mm closed seat; an older, longer seat would need trimming to 52 mm.</li>
        <li>Sight down the tool and confirm it sits dead on the centerline of the seat; a tool clamped off-center will give two different bevels.</li>
        <li>Hang the setter&rsquo;s <strong>inner hole B</strong> on the support, both discs on the stone, and lock.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p13_02.jpg" alt="Skew settings: seat angle scale set and locked with the bottom screw, protrusion against the setter, skew centered in the closed seat, and the setter in hole B with the support horizontal and vertical" />
        <figcaption>Figure 6-1: Closed-seat settings and the support set with hole B</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Shaping a Straight Edge</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Grade the stone coarse. Put the jig on the bar with the bevel on the stone and the long point toward you.</li>
        <li>Press with the thumb close to the edge, and push forward on the handle side &mdash; the wheel is trying to lift the tool off the bar in this position.</li>
        <li>To move across the stone, <strong>lift</strong> the tool and set it down in a new place rather than sliding it; sliding rounds the edge.</li>
        <li>A straight bevel is a large contact area and the stone glazes quickly. Re-activate it with the coarse side of the SP-650 every minute or so, or grind <strong>half the width</strong> of the bevel at a time to raise the pressure, then finish with the whole bevel on the stone.</li>
        <li>When the first bevel reaches the edge, <strong>turn the jig over</strong> and grind the second side. Keep going until the two bevels meet in a straight line and are symmetrical; if the first side needs more, go back to it.</li>
        <li>Check the protrusion before the last passes; shaping shortens the blade and P must be correct when the final geometry is cut.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p14_01.jpg" alt="Shaping a skew: lifting the tool to move sideways, pushing forward on the handle, and turning the jig over to grind the other side" />
        <figcaption>Figure 6-2: Lift to move, push on the handle, flip the jig for the second bevel</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p14_02.jpg" alt="Grinding half the width of a wide skew at a time to increase grinding pressure, then finishing with the entire bevel on the stone" />
        <figcaption>Figure 6-3: Half-width grinding for a wide bevel, then full-width to finish</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Shaping a Radius Edge</h3>
      <p>With the seat at 30&deg;, the curve is made by <strong>pivoting the jig on the bevelled corner</strong> of its base (marked A) as the tool crosses the stone; the trailing corner B lifts. The pivot is limited &mdash; the seat must stay in contact with the support bar throughout &mdash; and the long point is the part that disappears fastest, so watch it. A convex edge touches the stone on a small area, so the pressure is naturally high and the stone stays active without constant grading; the radius is actually quicker to shape than a straight edge. Flip and repeat on the other side until the bevels are symmetrical.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p15_01.jpg" alt="Straight edge with a large grinding area versus convex edge with a small grinding area" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p15_02.jpg" alt="Pivoting the jig on its bevelled corner A to create the curve with the maximum pivot shown; shaping panels and the finished radius skew profile" />
      </div>

      <h3>Sharpening</h3>
      <p>Set JS and P from the label, check the tool is centered, set the support with hole B, and give each bevel a few passes with the stone graded fine, flipping the jig between sides. For the radius skew, pivot to follow the curve. A minute, start to finish.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p14_03.jpg" alt="Settings for sharpening a skew: seat angle, bottom screw, protrusion, checking the tool is centered, and the setter in hole B" />
        <figcaption>Figure 6-4: Re-sharpening settings &mdash; the same three numbers off the label</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Honing Both Bevels</h3>
      <p>Leave the skew in the jig. Move the support to the honing side and set it with hole B just as for grinding. Hone one bevel, flip the jig, hone the other, and alternate until the wire edge is gone and both bevels are bright. The setter makes the honing angle identical to the grinding angle, which is why the honed band covers the whole bevel instead of a secondary micro-bevel.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p14_04.jpg" alt="Honing a skew: setter on the honing-wheel side, then honing each bevel alternately in the jig" />
        <figcaption>Figure 6-5: Honing both bevels in the jig</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Keeping a Non-Chart Skew</h3>
      <p>If a skew is to be kept at a geometry not on the chart (say a 25&deg; skew angle), mount it at 65 mm, draw a line across the stone with a pencil guided by the support bar, put the jig on the bar and rotate the seat until the existing edge is parallel to the line, lock the seat, and set the edge angle with the marker method. Record P and the seat angle on the label. Tormek&rsquo;s suggestion is that if the shape is close to a chart profile, convert it and save the setup time forever after.</p>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/rV7jznHhvLs" title="How to sharpen roughing gouges, skews & parting tools – Tormek SVS-50 Multi Jig – with Nick Agar" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>How to sharpen roughing gouges, skews &amp; parting tools &ndash; SVS-50 &ndash; with Nick Agar</em> (6:13)</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Parting and Beading Tools &mdash; SVS-50 Open Seat or SVD-110</h2>
      <p>Parting tools and beading tools have two symmetrical bevels ground square across the shank. In the <strong>open seat</strong> at 0&deg; they are treated like a roughing gouge that does not roll: grind one bevel, turn the jig over, grind the other. The Sorby 1/8&quot; is a thin rectangular blade; it clamps fine under the top screw, but if a blade is ever too thin or too short for the seat, the SVD-110 route below works for any parting tool.</p>
      <h3>In the SVS-50</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Open seat locked at 0&deg; against the stop. Support in the horizontal base.</li>
        <li>Protrusion <strong>75 mm</strong> off the setter; clamp with the top screw.</li>
        <li>Set the edge angle to the existing bevel with the marker method. (Tormek gives no fixed number here; a typical parting tool is around 40&ndash;50&deg; included.)</li>
        <li>Fingers near the edge; the other hand keeps the tool <strong>vertical</strong> on the bar, with the whole flange in contact. Grind until a burr forms, flip the jig, grind the second bevel, and compare: the two bevels should meet on the centerline of the blade. Touch up whichever side is short.</li>
        <li>Hone both bevels in the jig on the honing side, angle set with the marker method.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p18_01.jpg" alt="Parting and beading tool cross-sections, settings with the seat at zero degrees and 75 mm protrusion, grinding each bevel with the tool held vertical, checking symmetry, and honing in the jig" />
        <figcaption>Figure 7-1: Parting and beading tools in the open seat</figcaption>
      </div>
      <h3>Thin and Flat Parting Tools on the SVD-110</h3>
      <p>For a very thin blade, clamp the SVD-110 to the support, bring it close to the stone, and tilt it so the platform points slightly <em>below</em> the wheel&rsquo;s center &mdash; the offset should be about half the blade&rsquo;s thickness. Lay the blade flat on the rest and grind each bevel with fingers close to the edge so the wheel cannot pull the tool up. Fluted parting tools are done the same way, flute down on the rest.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p20_01.jpg" alt="Thin and fluted parting tool profiles, aligning the SVD-110 rest slightly below the wheel center, and grinding a thin parting tool and a fluted parting tool on the rest" />
        <figcaption>Figure 7-2: Thin parting tools on the SVD-110, rest aimed just under center</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h2>Scrapers &mdash; SVD-110 Tool Rest</h2>
      <p>The SVD-110 is a flat cast platform with a knob that clamps it to the universal support bar at any angle. The bore is wedge-shaped so the clamp bears on the sides of the bar rather than the bottom, which Tormek says multiplies the locking force by about 2.5 &mdash; in practice it means the rest does not creep under a heavy scraper. The platform is 90 &times; 110 mm; a long tool is fully supported, and for a short one the platform can be trimmed with a hacksaw to clear the handle.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/svd110_p01_01.jpg" alt="SVD-110 tool rest on the support with a scraper, next to the list of tools it handles: round nose, square end and curved scrapers, scorps, cabinet scrapers, hollowing tools and screwdrivers" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/svd110_p01_02.jpg" alt="Section through the SVD-110 clamp showing the tapered bore contacting the round bar on its sides" />
      </div>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/web_svd110_inuse.jpg" alt="Scraper being ground on the SVD-110 tool rest with the universal support vertical" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/web_svd110.jpg" alt="SVD-110 Tool Rest studio photo" />
      </div>

      <h3>Edge Angles and Support Position</h3>
      <p>Scrapers leave the factory at 70&ndash;80&deg; included. Some turners regrind to 60&deg; or even 45&deg; for a keener, shorter-lived edge. The angle decides where the support goes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Up to about 60&deg;:</strong> support in the <strong>vertical</strong> sleeves, grinding <em>toward</em> the edge. The wheel presses the tool down onto the rest and the tool can stay on the rest for honing.</li>
        <li><strong>Above about 60&deg;:</strong> support in the <strong>horizontal</strong> base, grinding <em>away</em> from the edge. A steep bevel ground toward the edge chatters; grinding away avoids it, but now the wheel is trying to lift the tool, so press it onto the rest and consider rounding the heel. The Sorby round nose (if kept at its factory angle), the Hurricane finishing scraper and the Henry Taylor NRS all live here.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p18_02.jpg" alt="Three panels: horizontal support for edge angles over about 60 degrees, and vertical support for angles up to about 60 degrees" />
        <figcaption>Figure 8-1: Support position by scraper edge angle</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Setting the Rest Angle</h3>
      <p>Lock the support, then tilt the rest on the bar. To match an existing bevel, color it and rock the wheel by hand with the scraper flat on the rest until the ink comes off evenly (marker method). To set a new angle, stand the WM-200 AngleMaster on the rest surface against the wheel and read the angle directly. Tighten the knob. If scrapers are sharpened often, a second universal support left permanently set up with the SVD-110 at the chosen angle removes the setup step entirely.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/svd110_p04_01.jpg" alt="Setting the SVD-110 angle: WM-200 AngleMaster on the rest to set a new angle, and the rest set to match an existing edge angle" />
        <figcaption>Figure 8-2: AngleMaster for a new angle; marker method to match the old one</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Grinding</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Lay the scraper flat on the rest with the bevel on the stone. Fingers close to the edge for control &mdash; the wheel is slow and wet and will not hurt them.</li>
        <li>For a round nose or a curved finishing scraper, swing the handle in an arc so the whole curve passes over the stone; keep the tool flat on the rest throughout. For a square-end scraper, slide it straight across.</li>
        <li>Use the full width of the stone. Stop when a burr can be felt along the whole edge on the top face.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p19_01.jpg" alt="Grinding a scraper on the SVD-110 with fingers close to the wheel, moving the rest to the honing wheel, and honing the top face freehand as a tangent to the wheel" />
        <figcaption>Figure 8-3: Grind, hone the bevel on the rest, then hone the top face freehand</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Burr, Hone, or Burnish</h3>
      <p>A scraper cuts with whatever is standing up at the edge. There are three ways to run it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Keep the grinding burr.</strong> The burr off a wet, slow wheel is clean steel with no embedded grit, so it is smoother and lasts longer than a dry-grinder burr. Go straight from the stone to the lathe. Re-burr by regrinding when it dulls.</li>
        <li><strong>Hone it off.</strong> Move the support and rest to the honing side, set the same angle, hone the bevel on the rest, then hone the top face freehand with the tool held tangent to the leather wheel. This gives a smooth square edge for a shear-scraping finishing cut.</li>
        <li><strong>Burnish a hook.</strong> After honing, clamp the scraper in the vise and draw a hard steel rod &mdash; the shank of a 12 mm HSS bowl gouge works &mdash; along the bevel tilted about <strong>5&deg;</strong> upward from the bevel line, with light pressure and 5&ndash;8 strokes. The edge rolls up into a tiny, polished hook that cuts cleanly and outlasts a grinding burr by a wide margin. More pressure is needed on a 70&ndash;80&deg; bevel than on a 60&deg; one. Too much pressure folds the hook backwards and it stops cutting; too large a hook makes the tool grabby.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p19_03.jpg" alt="Enlarged section of a scraper edge showing the micro-hook raised by burnishing at angle epsilon" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p19_02.jpg" alt="Burnishing a scraper clamped in a bench vise with a bowl gouge shank at about 5 degrees, and the resulting compressed shiny edge" />
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        Between regrinds a scraper can be re-burnished a few times; eventually the hook gets ragged and the fix is a fresh grind. For a finishing pass on the inside of a bowl, take the extra minute to hone bevel and top face before burnishing &mdash; the surface the hook leaves is only as good as the surfaces it was raised from.
      </div>

      <h3>Negative-Rake Scrapers</h3>
      <p>The Henry Taylor 1-1/2&quot; has a second, shallower bevel on the top face so the edge sits below the handle line and cannot self-feed. Tormek&rsquo;s literature does not cover this tool specifically; this is how it goes on the SVD-110:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Support horizontal, grinding away from the edge, as for any steep scraper.</li>
        <li>Lay the scraper bottom-down on the rest and set the rest angle to the <strong>lower (main) bevel</strong> with the marker method. Grind until a burr forms on the top.</li>
        <li>Turn the scraper over so the top face is down on the rest, re-set the rest angle to the <strong>upper bevel</strong> (again with the marker), and grind until the burr from step 2 is replaced by a fresh one pointing the other way. The last bevel ground decides which way the burr leans, so finish on whichever face you want the burr to stand proud of &mdash; conventionally the top.</li>
        <li>Go to the lathe with the burr on. Do not hone it off; the grinding burr is the cutting edge on an NRS, and with both bevels ground on the wet wheel it is a fine, clean one. Burnishing is optional and should be very light.</li>
        <li>Re-burr by repeating step 3 alone (upper bevel only) a couple of times before doing both bevels again.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/svd110_p04_02.jpg" alt="Tip from the SVD-110 instructions: trimming the rest surface so a short tool's handle clears it" />
        <figcaption>Figure 8-4: The platform may be trimmed for short-handled tools</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/ohzo2lSI0qw" title="How to sharpen scrapers and cutters – Tormek SVD-110 Tool Rest – with Nick Agar" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>How to sharpen scrapers and cutters &ndash; SVD-110 &ndash; with Nick Agar</em> (5:18)</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Carving Gouges and V-Tools</h2>
      <p>The SVD-186 R also handles carving gouges and V-tools, which is why the click ring runs all the way to 6: the lower numbers give the near-square sweep a carving gouge needs, the higher ones give long wings. The procedure follows the HB-10 rather than the TNT-10 and is only sketched here:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Mount the gouge as for a turning gouge, JS chosen for the sweep wanted; support in the vertical sleeves. Set the edge angle with the marker method, or by eye for a new angle &mdash; carving angles are a matter of the wood and the carver.</li>
        <li>Shape the edge profile first with the tool resting on the horizontal support close to the stone, then dress the blunted edge with the fine side of the stone grader so it shows a thin &ldquo;line of light&rdquo; when viewed edge-on. Grind the bevel in the jig until the line of light just disappears, checking often and removing the burr on the leather wheel each time so the burr is not mistaken for the line of light.</li>
        <li>Carving tools want <strong>sharp corners</strong>; do not round them off while swinging the jig.</li>
        <li>V-tools are ground as two flat bevels with the pivot locked by the knob (3), then the keel blended.</li>
        <li>Hone the inside on the LA-120 &mdash; the 60&deg; pointed disc fits a V-tool and small sweeps, the 3 mm radius disc the larger gouges.</li>
        <li>Wide, straight-shanked carving gouges (25&ndash;50 mm) and square carving chisels can instead go in the SVS-50 open seat; carving skews over about 105 mm long fit the closed seat.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/svd186_p03_02.jpg" alt="Three panels: the SVD-186 R click ring set 0 to 6, turning gouges with various wing lengths, and carving gouges and V-tools the jig can grind" />
        <figcaption>Figure 9-1: One jig setting range covers turning gouges, carving gouges and V-tools</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h2>Honing</h2>
      <p>Grinding leaves a wire edge and a matte bevel; honing removes the wire and polishes both faces, and a polished edge both cuts cleaner and stays sharp longer. The T-8&rsquo;s flat LA-220 leather wheel does the outside (bevel) of everything; the kit&rsquo;s LA-120 does the inside of flutes.</p>
      <div class="image-row">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/web_la120.jpg" alt="LA-120 profiled leather honing wheel mounted on the T-8 shaft beside the flat leather wheel" />
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/web_la120_inuse.jpg" alt="Gouge flute being honed on the LA-120 profiled wheel" />
      </div>

      <h3>LA-120 Setup</h3>
      <p>The LA-120 is a shaft (1) that threads into the honing-wheel end of the T-8&rsquo;s main shaft, with a spacer (2), flange (3), hub (4), washer (5) and a pair of leather discs (6) &mdash; one with a 3 mm radius, one with a 60&deg; point &mdash; stacked on it. On the T-8 the flat leather wheel is removed, the LA-120 shaft screwed on hand-tight, the flat wheel refitted, and the profiled discs tightened outboard of it. Before the first use, work a little light oil (sewing-machine oil) into the leather, then apply a thin line of PA-70 compound while turning the wheel by hand. A second set of narrow discs (LA-124: 2 mm radius and 45&deg; point) is sold separately for micro tools, and a second complete LA-120 can be stacked if both sets are wanted at once.</p>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p09_01.jpg" alt="Exploded drawing of the LA-120: shaft, spacer, flange, hub, washer and two exchangeable leather discs with 3 mm radius and 60 degree point profiles" />
        <figcaption>Figure 10-1: LA-120 components and disc profiles</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p09_02.jpg" alt="Preparing the LA-120: oiling the leather, applying PA-70 compound while turning by hand, and holding a gouge flute tangent to the disc" />
        <figcaption>Figure 10-2: Oil, compound, then present the flute tangent to the disc</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>Compound and Leather Care</h3>
      <p>The leather on both wheels needs oil once and compound regularly. Tormek&rsquo;s own figure for the flat LA-220 is about two-thirds of the supplied oil tube on a T-8 for the first impregnation; the small LA-120 discs need only a few drops. After that, one thin bead of PA-70 lasts roughly 5&ndash;10 tools. When the leather starts to look dry or the compound cakes, add a few drops of oil, a fresh bead of compound, and work it in by pressing a tool gently against the running wheel. Never let the compound dry out hard on the leather. The flat wheel&rsquo;s leather strip has a glued joint that is sanded flush at the factory; once the leather has compressed with use the joint can stand proud and tick against the tool, and a light pass with sandpaper on a block takes it back down.</p>
      <div class="warning">
        <strong>Always hone away from the edge.</strong> Presented edge-first, a tool cuts into the leather and can be snatched out of your hands. The burr is to be <em>honed</em> off gently, not snapped off &mdash; breaking it leaves micro-damage along the edge.
      </div>

      <h3>Technique</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Edge trailing, always.</strong> On the honing side the wheel surface moves away from the edge when the tool is presented from the universal support. Never turn a tool round so the edge faces into the leather &mdash; it will slice the wheel and can grab the tool.</li>
        <li><strong>Bevels:</strong> tool stays in its jig, support moves to the honing-side sleeves and is set with the same TTS-100 hole (or the marker method) as for grinding. Light pressure, a few strokes, swing or flip exactly as on the stone.</li>
        <li><strong>Flutes:</strong> out of the jig, flute laid tangent to the LA-120 disc, rocked from wing to wing. The disc should ride in the flute without the edge digging in. Alternate bevel and flute until no burr can be felt.</li>
        <li><strong>Scraper top faces:</strong> freehand, held tangent to the flat wheel.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/svd186_p04_02.jpg" alt="Three honing panels: setter positioned on the honing-wheel side, gouge in the SVD-186 R being honed on the flat leather wheel, and the flute on the LA-120" />
        <figcaption>Figure 10-4: Honing a gouge &mdash; setter, bevel in the jig, flute on the profiled wheel</figcaption>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>Small exchangeable cutters (not in this kit) should not be honed at all; they catch on the leather.</li>
        <li>Test with a push cut across end grain on a scrap; the fibres should sever cleanly. Because the jig and support are untouched, going back for a few more strokes costs nothing.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p09_03.jpg" alt="Section views of a small gouge and a 32 mm roughing gouge between the two LA-120 discs, showing the clearance between the discs" />
        <figcaption>Figure 10-3: The disc spacing takes everything from a detail gouge to a 32 mm roughing gouge</figcaption>
      </div>
      <div class="video">
        <div class="frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/BzN82T8e-TA" title="Tormek Profiled Leather Honing Wheel LA-120" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"></iframe></div>
        <p class="caption">Tormek: <em>Profiled Leather Honing Wheel LA-120</em> (1:11)</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Grading the Stone for Turning Tools</h2>
      <p>The SG-250 Original Grindstone is nominally 220 grit, but the SP-650 Stone Grader changes its working surface: the coarse side knocks it back to a fast-cutting 220, the fine side burnishes it to about 1000. Press the grader on the running stone for 30&ndash;45 seconds, moving it across the full width; the corners of the coarse side work best for re-activating. (Diamond wheels are not graded.) For turning tools:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Coarse</strong> for every shaping job, for a chipped edge, and whenever a big flat bevel &mdash; a straight skew especially &mdash; starts to glaze and stop cutting. Re-activate mid-job as often as needed.</li>
        <li><strong>Fine</strong> for touch-ups of spindle gouges, skews, detail tools and anything used for a finishing cut, where almost no steel needs to come off and a finer scratch pattern hones out faster.</li>
        <li>Bowl gouges and roughing gouges are usually fine on the coarse surface; they get honed afterwards anyway.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="figure">
        <img src="/manuals/tormek-tnt-808/tnt10_p16_01.jpg" alt="Grading for fine grinding with the fine side of the SP-650 pressed on the stone, and returning to fast grinding with the coarse side" />
        <figcaption>Figure 11-1: Fine side for touch-ups, coarse side to return to fast grinding</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h3>The Ten-Second Touch-Up</h3>
      <p>Tormek&rsquo;s whole argument for the setter is that a mid-session sharpening should be shorter than the walk to the grinder. Once the tool has been shaped and labelled, the loop is:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Read the label. Set JS on the jig.</li>
        <li>Drop the tool into the jig and set P on side 2 of the setter. Clamp.</li>
        <li>Hook the setter&rsquo;s hole A or B over the support, discs on the stone, lock the support.</li>
        <li>Swing (gouge), roll (roughing gouge) or flip (skew) across the stone for 10&ndash;20 seconds with light pressure until a burr shows.</li>
        <li>Shift the support to the honing side, set it with the same hole, hone the bevel; strip the burr from the flute on the LA-120.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Two or three minutes including honing, and the edge comes back at exactly the shape that was turning well before.</p>
